{
  "name": "vector_light_gap_vs_door",
  "saveFile": "minimal_colony.rws",
  "description": "Does light spilling outdoors through a GAP look like light spilling outdoors through a DOOR? Two identical roofed rooms in ONE frame, one torch each, each torch four cells from its own west opening onto the same open ground. The only difference between them is one wall cell: the north room's opening holds a wooden door, the south room's is bare floor. That single cell is the whole independent variable, and it changes the composition's input completely -- RimWorld's glow grid floods straight through a gap and NEVER learns a door opened, so the same lamp at the same distance presents a large vanilla term in one case and exactly zero in the other. The vanilla arm is where that asymmetry is measured: the ground west of the gap is lit and the ground west of the door is not, with no mod running at all. Arms: vanilla, the shader max as shipped, the surface lift, and the shader max again as a same-build control for the run-to-run pixel floor. Gravel everywhere so the readable texture inside each beam is comparable between the two openings and between the arms; every cloud flag off and stated, because the whole measured region is open ground. NOTE that a gap makes its room OUTDOOR as far as RimWorld is concerned however thoroughly it is roofed, so the two rooms' INTERIORS are not comparable and nothing here compares them -- the measurement is on the open ground west of both openings. Two diagnostic arms follow the control and are the reason this scenario can say WHICH half is responsible: mask-only (the shader stood down, so only suppression and its restoration run) and no-suppress (vanilla left intact underneath). Past a gap the max has nothing to add because vanilla already delivered our own value, so anything the ground loses there is the restoring half rather than the drawing half.",
  "hideUi": true,
